{"conditionalSetIds":[],"forecasterId":"demographics-ai-2036:run1:growth:outside","informationSetArtifactId":"sha256:0126e3013e992fa67d8e5280469c56d5cb8616cacb2e8073432d1de0b266dff3","modelForecastIds":[],"modelUse":"none","prediction":{"kind":"binary","positiveOutcomeId":"yes","probability":0.33},"questionHash":"sha256:cfe468fa9439bedeee3804a35583667745f13831322e2c91a89c02a1b211413b","questionId":"demographics-ai-2036-cq5","questionVersion":"1.0.0","rationale":"Conjunct A (ICE BofA MOVE 10-year average >=105, 2027-2036). Mandated reference class: MOVE since 1988. CAVEAT, stated per the discipline rule -- I attempted to retrieve verified annual averages this session and could NOT; public search returned only per-year open/close/high/low (ConvexTrade, 2002-2026) and the 1988 start date, no annual means. The following are my own recollected annual averages and should be treated as unverified: late-1980s/1990s ~95-115; 1995-2004 ~95-105; 2008 ~175; 2000-2009 ~105; 2010-2019 ~75 (2017 ~52, 2019 ~65); 2020 ~70; 2021 ~62; 2022 ~122; 2023 ~122; 2024 ~95-100; 2025 ~75-85 (consistent with the retrieved 2025 open 96.67 / close 63.96 and 2023 open 129.97 / close 114.62). Of ~28 rolling 10-year windows (1988-1997 through 2016-2025), those averaging >=105 are roughly the earliest two and the 1999-2008/2000-2009 pair: 5-8 of 28, base rate ~20-28%. Mechanical adjustment: MOVE is a basis-point (not proportional) vol measure, so it is arithmetically higher when the level of rates is higher -- the >=105 windows are precisely the higher-rate-level windows, and the locked starting point is a 2.60% US real yield rather than the ~0% of 2012-2021. Conjunct A = 45%. Conjunct B (>=1 episode of MSCI World -25% peak-to-trough with G7 GDP-weighted 10y nominal failing to fall >=50bp, or rising >=25bp, in the drawdown window). Two nested base rates. (i) Frequency of a >=25% MSCI World drawdown inside a 10-year window: episodes since 1970 are 1973-74, 1980-82, 1987, ~1990, 2000-02, 2007-09, 2020, 2022. Almost every 10-year window contains one; the clear exceptions are 1991-1999 and 2010-2019 (2011 ~-22%, 2015-16 ~-19%, 2018 ~-18% all fell short). Base rate ~85-88%. (ii) CONDITIONAL on such a drawdown, frequency that bonds failed to rally: 1973-74 yields rose (YES), 1981-82 yields rose through most of the drawdown (YES), Aug-Oct 1987 yields rose ~150bp into the crash (YES, marginal), 1990 G7-weighted fell only ~40bp with JGB yields rising (ambiguous, score 0.5), 2000-02 US 10y 6.5%->3.6% (NO), 2007-09 sharp rally (NO), 2020 sharp rally (NO), 2022 yields rose ~200bp (YES). So ~3.5-4 of 8 = ~45%. That is the central historical fact for this question: the flight-to-quality bid is not a law, it is a low-inflation-regime property, and it failed in every inflation-driven or supply-driven drawdown. With ~1.4 expected qualifying drawdowns per 10-year window: P = 0.87 * [1-(1-0.45)^1.4] ~ 0.87*0.58 ~ 0.50; I mark to 48% for the ambiguity in the 1987 and 1990 codings. Correlation: strongly positive, rho = +0.45 -- a high-MOVE decade and a failed-flight-to-quality decade are the same regime (1970s-80s, 2022), and a low-MOVE decade mechanically produces the working flight-to-quality bid (2000-02, 2008, 2020). Joint = 0.45*0.48 + 0.45*sqrt(0.45*0.55*0.48*0.52) = 0.216 + 0.45*0.2486 = 0.216 + 0.112 = 0.328. CQ5 = 33. I note without pricing it that the locked ~$1.4T circular commitments / ~$650B debt issuance and the +2.4 pp Debt-at-Risk sensitivity to a 20% AI-led equity shock describe exactly the levered-basis-trade transmission channel that converts an equity drawdown into a Treasury selloff -- but that is a mechanism specific to this episode, so my lens forbids me from adjusting on it.","referenceClassIds":[],"schemaVersion":"forecast-workbench.forecast/v1","sealedAt":"2026-11-04T17:31:54.135Z","seriesId":"published-panel","strongestContraryCase":"Not elicited per question. Panelist falsification signposts: {\"signpost\":\"OECD area harmonised unemployment rate (FRED OECDLRHUTTTTSTSAQ / OECD MEI) printing a 12-month average at or below 4.6% with no OECD-area recession dated in the interval -- i.e. the demographic labour-scarcity floor arriving faster than the cyclical reference class allows.\",\"by_when\":\"Dec 31, 2029\",\"if_observed_implies\":\"CQ1 conjunct A moves from 45% toward 65-70%, lifting CQ1 from 16 to roughly 24-26. It also raises CQ2 conjunct A (wage-driven services inflation) by ~5 points and shifts CQ6 mass from D toward B and C by ~4 points combined. It does NOT move CQ3, since tighter labour with a shrinking headcount is the same output.\"} | {\"signpost\":\"A full 5-year realised print of PPP-weighted (OECD-38 + China) real GDP growth for 2026-2030 at or above 2.6%/yr on IMF WEO constant-PPP data, with China's own 2028-2030 average at or above 4.0%/yr.\",\"by_when\":\"Apr 30, 2032 (IMF WEO publishing the 2031 actual)\",\"if_observed_implies\":\"This is the cleanest falsifier of my central claim, because it would show the China-weight-times-deceleration arithmetic I leaned on by ~110bp is not binding. CQ3 P50 moves from 1.9% to ~2.5% and the whole distribution shifts up ~60bp; CQ6-A moves from 19 to ~34, B stays ~47, C and D fall to ~11 and ~8. My entire departure from the 85-90% historical base rate for band A would be invalidated.\"} | {\"signpost\":\"The G7 GDP-weighted 10y sovereign real yield (US DFII10 plus euro-area, UK, Japan and Canada 10y nominal less 10y inflation swap) averaging below 1.0% over any rolling 24 months, with US net interest outlays still at or above 3% of GDP in the same period.\",\"by_when\":\"Dec 31, 2031\",\"if_observed_implies\":\"Falsifies my persistence-plus-supply-tilt anchor: it would show the debt arithmetic does not transmit to real yields (convenience yield intact, or a renewed central-bank duration bid). CQ4 P50 moves from 2.1% to ~1.3% and P90 from 3.4% to ~2.6%; CQ5 conjunct A (MOVE >=105) falls from 45% to ~32%, dragging CQ5 from 33 to ~24; CQ6-D rises ~5 points at C's expense. Asset matrix: G7 long nominal bonds in regime C would need marking up and gold marking down.\"} | {\"signpost\":\"An MSCI World peak-to-trough decline of 25% or more during which G7 GDP-weighted 10y nominal yields fall by 100bp or more -- i.e. the flight-to-quality bid working cleanly through a large drawdown in a post-2026 high-debt, high-real-rate setting.\",\"by_when\":\"Dec 31, 2031\",\"if_observed_implies\":\"Consumes the most likely qualifying episode of the window with a NO. CQ5 conjunct B falls from 48% to roughly 28% (only a later, second drawdown could qualify), taking CQ5 from 33 to ~20. It also raises the equity-bond diversification value underpinning my regime-C/regime-D bond spread, narrowing the C-vs-D gap for G7 long nominals from 10 pp to ~6 pp.\"} | {\"signpost\":\"OECD or Eurostat structural business/earnings statistics showing cumulative real median hourly earnings growth in ISIC F+Q exceeding ISIC J+K+M by 5.0 pp or more over 2025-2031 across a majority of the OECD-38 by GDP weight (i.e. half the required 11-year differential delivered in six years).\",\"by_when\":\"Dec 31, 2032 (on data through 2031)\",\"if_observed_implies\":\"CQ1 conjunct B moves from 22% to ~55%, taking CQ1 from 16 to roughly 30-33. It also raises CQ6-B at A's and D's expense (the barbell's defining wage signature) by ~5 points, and raises the locked AI-Q5 marginal's implied value further above 38.2%. Note this signpost's absence by 2032 would NOT confirm my view, since the differential could be back-loaded.\"}","updateBasis":{"kind":"launch","note":"run1 wave 1 outside seat, effort 'growth'."}}